**All 6 Relational Operators (Return boolean):**

```java
int x = 10, y = 20;

// 1. Equal to (==)
System.out.println(x == y);   // false
System.out.println(10 == 10); // true

// 2. Not equal to (!=)
System.out.println(x != y);   // true
System.out.println(10 != 10); // false

// 3. Greater than (>)
System.out.println(x > y);    // false
System.out.println(20 > 10);  // true

// 4. Less than (<)
System.out.println(x < y);    // true
System.out.println(20 < 10);  // false

// 5. Greater than or equal to (>=)
System.out.println(x >= y);   // false
System.out.println(10 >= 10); // true

// 6. Less than or equal to (<=)
System.out.println(x <= y);   // true
System.out.println(10 <= 10); // true
```

**Return Type - Always boolean:**
```java
boolean result1 = (5 > 3);     // true
boolean result2 = (10 == 20);  // false
boolean result3 = (x <= y);    // depends on values

// Cannot assign to int
// int wrong = (5 > 3);  // ERROR! boolean cannot be int
```

**Use in Conditions:**
```java
int age = 18;

if (age >= 18) {
    System.out.println("Can vote");
}

int score = 85;
if (score >= 60) {
    System.out.println("Passed");
}
```

**Common Use Cases:**
```java
// Age verification
int age = 25;
boolean isAdult = age >= 18;  // true

// Range check
int score = 75;
boolean isPassing = score >= 60;  // true

// Equality check
int count = 10;
boolean isEmpty = count == 0;  // false

// Comparison
int price1 = 100, price2 = 150;
boolean isCheaper = price1 < price2;  // true
```

**String Comparison (IMPORTANT!):**
```java
// WRONG - Use == for strings
String s1 = "Hello";
String s2 = "Hello";
if (s1 == s2) { }  // Unreliable! Compares references

// CORRECT - Use .equals() for strings
if (s1.equals(s2)) { }  // Correct! Compares content
```

**Chaining Comparisons (WRONG!):**
```java
// WRONG - Cannot chain like math
int x = 15;
// if (10 < x < 20) { }  // ERROR! Invalid syntax

// CORRECT - Use logical AND
if (x > 10 && x < 20) { }  // Correct!
```

**Operator Precedence:**
```java
int a = 5, b = 10, c = 3;

// Arithmetic before relational
boolean result = a + b > c * 2;  // (5 + 10) > (3 * 2) → 15 > 6 → true

// Use parentheses for clarity
boolean result = (a + b) > (c * 2);  // Same, but clearer
```

**Comparison Table:**

| Operator | Meaning | Example | Result |
|----------|---------|---------|--------|
| == | Equal to | 5 == 5 | true |
| != | Not equal | 5 != 3 | true |
| > | Greater than | 5 > 3 | true |
| < | Less than | 5 < 3 | false |
| >= | Greater or equal | 5 >= 5 | true |
| <= | Less or equal | 5 <= 3 | false |

